# Generated from: specs/400-restore-per-spec-plan-approval-and-ci-gated-merge-waiting/requirements.md
Feature: Restore per-spec plan approval and CI-gated merge waiting
  Write-spec previously reused the first approval for continuation issues,
  and terminal delivery could leave CI observation before gates were terminal.

  @SCN001 @regression
  Scenario: Every published spec returns to native plan before continuation
    Given an approved write-spec package has merged and post-merge remediation has completed
    When the publication execution turn settles
    Then the extension dispatches exactly one follow-up beginning with /plan
    And Continue or Finished is presented only after that native-plan re-entry
    And a publication result without merged true dispatches no follow-up

  @SCN002 @regression
  Scenario: A continuation issue has its own approval boundary
    Given issue N is recorded in the write-spec session published list
    When the operator selects eligible issue M and read-only discovery completes
    Then local://spec-{M}-plan.md contains the complete four-file package for M
    And xd://propose is called for M before any branch, file, commit, push, pull-request, label, or merge mutation for M
    And leaving that proposal performs no mutation for M

  @SCN003 @regression
  Scenario: Incomplete CI remains observable without a deadline
    Given terminal delivery is bound to pull request P at exact head H
    And a required check is not registered, pending, or represented by a CI-attributable BLOCKED state
    When delivery evaluates readiness
    Then it observes again after 30 seconds without a workflow deadline
    And it does not merge, remediate, consume merge recovery, or write a failed handoff for that non-terminal state

  @SCN004 @regression
  Scenario: Terminal evidence controls exact-head delivery
    Given terminal delivery remains bound to pull request P at exact head H
    When all required checks succeed and a fresh snapshot is CLEAN
    Then it issues one squash merge with --match-head-commit H
    And it reports success only after P is MERGED at H and the linked issue is CLOSED
    But when a check fails or a proven non-CI blocker remains
    Then it uses the existing remediation or failed-handoff route without classifying that state as pending
